00:00:02.149 --> 00:00:03.669 align:start position:0%
police in france have arrested a third  suspect in connection with a deadly  terror attack in nice  authorities say a suspected terrorist  stabbed and killed three people  inside a catholic church on thursday the  attack comes just two weeks after a  teacher was beheaded  in a paris suburb france has raised its  security alert to its highest level  president emmanuel macron called the  attacks islamist acts of terror  his remarks have led to tens of tens of  thousands of muslims to protest in the  middle east  mts tayab is in paris and joins us mts  good morning  well good morning the streets of paris  are eerily empty as france enters the  second day  of a strict covid 19 lockdown and as  many come to terms with yet  another brutal attack  across france church bells ring in honor  of those who were killed  and as mourners continue to pay their  respects new details are emerging  about the attacker investigators say his  name is ibrahim asawi  a 21-year-old tunisian national who  entered europe just over a month ago  and is now in critical condition after  being shot by police  his mother who lives in tunisia says he  called her just days ago to tell her she  was in france  saying only that she should pray for him  since last month there's been three  separate assaults connected to cartoons  depicting the prophet muhammad that were  first published in 2015  by the satirical magazine charlie hebdo  and were republished again  this september the images  seen as a gratuitous insult to islam  have sparked protests across several  muslim nations  much of the fury is pointed directly at  president macron  whose defense of the crude caricatures  as part of france's secular values  has opened him up to accusations of  deliberately stoking  anti-muslim sentiment for political gain  a strategy that may have backfired  internationally  but seems to be winning influence at  home despite the staggering cost  now president macron has more than  doubled the number of troops on the  streets to over 7  thousand with many here fearing more  violence  michelle mts tayyip in paris  thank you
 

